Backend Architecture
Version: 2026-07-17
Status: Authoritative Backend Guide

Overview
This document explains the backend architecture of WordList Writer. It describes how Express is structured, how API routes connect to controllers, how Supabase is integrated, and how the save and load pipelines operate. It is designed for future development sessions and debugging backend issues.

1. High-Level Structure
The backend is a lightweight Express server. It provides:
* static file hosting
* API routes for saving and loading data
* Supabase integration for persistence
* middleware for JSON parsing and routing

The backend does not perform text analysis. All analysis occurs in the frontend.

2. Server Entry Point
The backend entry point is:
src/server.js

Responsibilities:
* initialize Express
* register middleware
* register API routes
* serve static files
* start HTTP server

3. Middleware Order
Middleware order is critical:
* JSON body parser
* API routes
* static file hosting

Static hosting must come last. If placed earlier, it will shadow API routes.

4. API Route Structure
API routes live in:
src/routes

Each route file:
* defines endpoints
* imports controllers
* exports router

Example route types:
* /api/project/save
* /api/project/load
* /api/master/save
* /api/master/load

5. Controllers
Controllers live in:
src/controllers

Responsibilities:
* validate request data
* call Supabase client
* transform results
* return JSON responses

Controllers must not contain business logic. They only coordinate operations.

6. Supabase Client
Supabase client lives in:
src/lib/supabase.js

Responsibilities:
* create Supabase client instance
* expose client for controllers

Environment variables required:
* SUPABASE_URL
* SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY

7. Database Tables
Supabase stores:
* projects
* project_wordlists
* master_wordlists
* cross_language_master

Full schema is documented in:
docs/Supabase_Schema.md

8. SaveEverything Workflow
SaveEverything is the core backend workflow.

Steps:
* save project metadata
* capture returned project id
* save project wordlist
* save master list
* return success response

This order must never change.

9. Load Workflow
Load workflow restores project state.

Steps:
* load project metadata
* load project wordlist
* load master list
* return combined JSON

Frontend is responsible for rebuilding UI state.

10. Error Handling
Backend error handling rules:
* always return JSON errors
* never crash server
* log Supabase errors
* validate request bodies
* validate project id

11. Static File Hosting
Static files live in:
public/

Server uses:
express.static(\"public\")

Static hosting must be registered after API routes.

12. Adding New API Routes
When adding new routes:
* create route file in src/routes
* create controller in src/controllers
* update API_Specification.md
* update Docs_Index.md

13. Safe Coding Practices
* never mix analysis logic into backend
* always validate inputs
* always check Supabase errors
* always return JSON
* keep controllers small and focused
* keep route files simple

14. Summary
This backend architecture document explains how Express is structured, how API routes and controllers work, how Supabase is integrated, and how save and load pipelines operate. It is essential for debugging backend issues and safely extending backend functionality.

